 ## Overview
-Mesh BVH is a GPU-accelerated bounding volume hierarchy designed for triangle meshes to perform efficient distance queries, serving as the geometric backend for signed distance field (SDF) representations in volume rendering pipelines [S1, unofficial]. It enables fast evaluation of point-to-mesh distance, closest point location, and topological properties (sign, winding number) required for ray-marching or grid-based rendering [S1, unofficial]. Unlike hierarchical voxel traversals that operate on implicit grid structures, this method operates directly on explicit triangle geometry, making it suitable for high-frequency surface details that would be prohibitive to store in a dense or even sparse voxel grid [S1, unofficial].
+Mesh BVH is a GPU-accelerated bounding volume hierarchy designed for efficient distance queries and sign evaluation on triangle meshes [S1, unofficial]. It solves the performance bottleneck of testing all triangles in large meshes by enabling hierarchical pruning during ray-mesh intersection or point-to-mesh distance calculations [S1, unofficial]. It is distinguished from its siblings by its specific integration into the `isoext.sdf.TriangleMeshSDF` pipeline for signed distance field computation, prioritizing raw speed for refractive path searches over guaranteed convergence properties found in interval-based refinement methods [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method constructs a static BVH over the input triangle mesh vertices and faces, stored in CUDA tensors for GPU execution [S1, unofficial]. The hierarchy allows for culling of non-intersecting triangles during distance queries [S1, unofficial].
-
